We propose an efficient, adaptable skeleton-based method for converting an irregularly shaped point set into a network-friendly format suitable for training. The key ideas are:
(1) The pyramid-like skeleton offers additional semantic information compared to traditional skeletons and multi-layer point sets;
(2) There are three situations where the standard skeleton is insufficient: instances with multiple separated regions, instances defined by multiple closed curves, and instances with isolated points that don't fit a single loop or pyramid structure;
(3) In these cases, we extract supplementary skeletons to enhance understanding of the point set geometry;
(4) We utilize a novel pyramid-like skeleton representation to supervise the generation of a compact point set that facilitates efficient learning through a dynamic point allocator.
